d by a virus” (singular). This is a simplification that avoids the complex reality of 200+ strains, just like we might say “bacteria cause strep throat” without naming every strain of *Streptococcus*. The problem is, people then carry forward the idea that there’s a specific “cold virus” that can just be cured, like the measles virus. So, to be crystal clear: **Your original thought that there was one virus is what most people think. My explanation was trying to correct that hidden, widely-held assumption.** The “convenience” of the name is just the linguistic outcome of the biological trick these hundreds of viruses play on us by all causing the same symptoms. In other words, you were right to think it's strange that we call it one thing—and the reason it feels strange is that our language hasn't caught up with the fact that "the common cold" is a description of a shared experience, not a specific infection.
